  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Three straight wins, all by one run. That's not sustainable, and everyone in that clubhouse knows it.",
    "what_this_means": "Casey Mize carved through six innings on two runs and six strikeouts. The kind of start that should win most games. The offense gave him almost nothing to work with until the seventh inning, when Jackson Merrill flipped a 2-1 deficit with a two-run homer. That swing was the game. Ty France added another run earlier with his own power, but Merrill's timing in the seventh made the difference between survival and victory. The bullpen. Yuki Matsui, Adrian Morejon, and closer Mason Miller. Combined for three scoreless innings with no hiccups. It was clean, compact, professional. The Padres had their chances in the third with runners in scoring position and got nothing. Then they waited. Late power finally arrived. This is how they've won three straight: lean on pitching, strike late, hold on. It works until someone adjusts.",
    "what_to_watch": "Kyle Harrison comes in tonight for Milwaukee with a 2.84 ERA. The kind of pitcher who thrives when lineups are impatient. Walker Buehler takes the ball for San Diego, and if he can match Mize's length, another late game could be coming.",
